# Example environment for the afterlife container.
#
#   install -m 0600 deploy/afterlife.env.example /etc/afterlife/env
#   $EDITOR /etc/afterlife/env
#   podman run --env-file /etc/afterlife/env ...
#
# This is a podman --env-file, NOT a shell script. Values are taken
# literally: no `export`, no interpolation, and no quoting — writing
#   SMTP_PASS="hunter2"
# makes the quotes part of the password. Leave values bare.
#
# Keep the real file at mode 0600 and outside this repo.

# --- Required: the release refuses to boot without these ---------------

# mix phx.gen.secret
SECRET_KEY_BASE=replace-me-64-plus-random-characters

# mix afterlife.gen_cloak_key
#
# Decrypts every stored message. Back this up somewhere OTHER than the
# host and the database backup — with the DB but no key, every message
# is unrecoverable ciphertext. Rotating it strands existing messages.
CLOAK_KEY=replace-me-base64-32-byte-key

# SQLite file, inside the mounted volume (/data/afterlife on the host,
# see deploy/afterlife.nix). Must be on the volume, or the database dies
# with the container.
DATABASE_PATH=/data/afterlife.db

# SMTP relay. Every feature here is an email, so this is not optional.
SMTP_RELAY=smtp.maileroo.com

# --- Optional ----------------------------------------------------------

SMTP_PORT=587
SMTP_USER=you@example.com
SMTP_PASS=replace-me
EMAIL_FROM=afterlife@example.com

# Public hostname, used for the check-in links in reminder emails. Get
# this wrong and the links in the emails point somewhere useless.
PHX_HOST=afterlife.example.com
PORT=4000

# --- Monitoring -------------------------------------------------------
#
# There's nothing to configure here. Point an external uptime monitor at
#
#     https://<PHX_HOST>/health
#
# and alert on any non-200. That endpoint reports the age of the last
# completed sweep, so it goes 503 when the scheduler stops even though
# the site is still serving — which is the failure that matters
# (docs/DESIGN.md §0).
#
# The monitor must live OFF this host: one that dies with the machine it
# watches tells you nothing.

# Only for multi-node clustering, which SQLite rules out here. Leave unset.
# DNS_CLUSTER_QUERY=
